          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "A fair die is rolled 20 times, and an odd number is rolled every time.",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "break"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Explain why the ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "21^{\\mathrm{st}}",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " roll is equally likely to be either odd or even.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Each roll is independent, so previous results do not affect the next roll. Odd and even each have probability $\\frac12$.",
      "worked": "Each roll is independent, so the previous 20 odd results do not affect the 21st roll. A fair standard die has three odd faces and three even faces.\n$$\\begin{align*}P(\\text{odd})&=\\frac36=\\frac12\\\\P(\\text{even})&=\\frac36=\\frac12\\end{align*}$$\nThe 21st roll is equally likely to be odd or even.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 28
  },
